\h Heroes and Guides
\toc1 Various Heroes and Guides
\toc2 Heroes and Guides
\toc3 Ldrs
\mt2 The accounts of
\mt1 Various Heroes and Guides
\mt3 commonly called ‘Judges’
\is1 Introduction
\ip This document commonly called ‘\bk Judges\bk*’, contains a collection of accounts of ‘\bk Various Heroes and Guides/Leaders\bk*’ who helped to rescue the Israeli people from their oppressors. It covers the times from their invasion of Canaan through to just before their first king. Most of these heroes or guides were actually warriors. Shimshon (commonly called ‘Samson’) is the best-known of all of them, and his story can be read in chapters 13–16.
\ip From this document we can learn that Yisrael (‘Israel’ in older English translations) prospered when they obeyed Yahweh's instructions, but when they disobeyed, then God allowed other peoples and nations to oppress them. However, God is always ready and prepared to save his people whenever they turn away from their disobedience and return again to obeying the instructions that he'd given them.
\ip The previous book was named after the main character Yehoshua (Joshua) and the following books (Ruth and 1 Shemuel/Samuel) are named after their main characters, but this document with somewhere around a dozen important characters, has no single prominent character that it could be named after. Instead it's traditionally named ‘Judges’ in English because of around twenty uses of the Hebrew root ‘שָׁפַט’ (shafat) often in roots normally translated as ‘judged’. Despite that, there's not a single account in this document of these leaders judging the cases and conflicts of the common people. This dilemma offers two possible interpretations: 1/ that these leaders (often military leaders) did judge cases for the common people, but those details aren't recorded, or 2/ (which we've leaned more towards) that this Hebrew word has a wider range of meaning, and so we've leaned towards the English word ‘guided’ or ‘led’ in these contexts.
\iot Main components of this account
\io1 The events up until Yehoshua's death \ior 1:1–2:10\ior*
\io1 The various heroes/guides of Yisrael \ior 2:11–16:31\ior*
\io1 Various other events \ior 17:1–21:25\ior*
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\ie
\c 1
\s1 Yehudah and Simeon capture Adoni-Bezek
\rem /s1 Completing the conquest of Canaan; Israel Fights the Remaining Canaanites; Israel's Failure to Complete the Conquest of Canaan; Judah and Simeon Conquer the Land; The Tribes of Judah and Simeon Capture Adonibezek
\p
\v 1 After¦111578 Yehoshua's¦111580 death¦111579, the Israelis¦111583 asked Yahweh¦111584, “\add ≈Which tribe should go¦111587 first¦111593 to attack the Canaanites¦111592\add*?”
\v 2 “Yehudah¦111599 will¦111600 go¦111600 first,” Yahweh¦111598 \add ≈answered¦111597\add*. “Listen¦111601, I've¦111602 \add ≈already determined that they'll conquer\add* the¦111605 land¦111605.”
\p
\v 3 Then the Yehudah¦111609 \add leaders\add* said¦111608 to¦111610 \add the tribe of\add* Simeon¦111610, “\add ≈Come¦111612\add* with¦111614 \add #us\add* into the region that¦111615 we've been allocated and¦111608 we can fight¦111615 together against¦111616 the Canaanites¦111616. Then we'll do the same in¦111614 your¦111622 area.” So¦111608 \add ≈the warriors from the two tribes worked together\add*.
\v 4 When¦111628 \add *they\add* attacked, Yahweh¦111630 \add ≈gave¦111629 them victory over\add* the Canaanites¦111633 and the Perizzites¦111634 and they killed ten¦111638 thousand¦111639 men¦111640 at¦111635 Bezek¦111637.
\v 5 They¦111642 \add ≈confronted\add* Adoni¦111645-Bezek in¦111647 Bezek and fought¦111648 against him¦111649 (then¦111642 eventually defeating¦111650 the Canaanites¦111653 and the Perizzites¦111656).
\v 6 Adoni¦111659-Bezek¦111660 \add tried to\add* flee¦111658 but¦111658 they¦111661 chased him and captured him, cutting¦111665 off¦111665 his thumbs¦111668 and big¦111670 toes.
\v 7 “Seventy¦111676 kings¦111678 had their thumbs¦111679 and big¦111681 toes chopped off¦111682,” Adoni¦111673-Bezek¦111675 said¦111672, “and were¦111683 gathering¦111684 scraps of¦111686 food beneath my¦111686 table¦111686. Just¦111687 as¦111687 I¦111688 have¦111688 done¦111688, so¦111689 God¦111693 has¦111690 repaid¦111690 to me¦111692.” Then¦111672 they¦111694 took him¦111694 to Yerushalem¦111695 and he¦111696 died¦111696 there¦111697.
\s1 The capture of Yerushalem and Hebron
\rem /s1 The Tribe of Judah Conquers Jerusalem and Hebron
\p
\v 8 The¦111701 warriors from Yehudah¦111703 attacked Yerushalem¦111704 and captured¦111705 the city, killing the inhabitants and setting the city on¦111704 fire¦111715.
\v 9 Then¦111717 they¦111718 fought¦111721 against¦111722 the Canaanites¦111722 who¦111723 lived in the hill¦111724 country¦111724, in the southern \add wilderness\add*, and¦111717 in the \add western\add* foothills.
\v 10 \add *They\add* also fought the Canaanites¦111732 in¦111734 Kiriath¦111740-Arba (now known as Hebron¦111734) and killed Sheshai¦111744, Ahiman¦111747, and Talmai¦111750.
\s1 Otniel captures Debir
\rem /s1 Othniel Conquers the City of Debir
\r (Josh. 15:13-19)
\p
\v 11 From¦111753 there¦111753 \add #they\add* \add ≈fought against¦111754\add* Kiriath¦111764-Sepher¦111764 (now known as Debir¦111757).
\v 12 Caleb¦111767 \add had\add* said¦111766, “The¦111768 one who¦111768 attacks¦111770 Kiriath¦111773-Sepher¦111775 and¦111766 captures¦111776 it \add ≈can marry\add* my¦111782 daughter¦111782 Aksah¦111781.”
\v 13 Otniel¦111786 (son¦111787 of Caleb's¦111791 younger brother¦111790 Kenaz¦111789) captured¦111785 it¦111793 so \add @Caleb\add* gave¦111794 his daughter¦111800 Aksah¦111799 to him¦111796 to become his wife.
\v 14 \add ≈After the wedding\add*, she urged¦111805 \add @Otniel\add* to ask¦111806 her father¦111809 for a field¦111810. When¦111803 she got off her donkey¦111813, Caleb¦111817 asked her, “What¦111818 can I do for you¦111820?”
\v 15 “Give¦111832 me a blessing¦111827,” she¦111822 \add ≈requested\add*. “Although you've¦111831 given¦111831 me land¦111829 in the southern wilderness, give¦111832 me some springs of¦111834 water¦111835 as well.” So¦111822 Caleb¦111839 gave¦111836 her¦111838 the upper¦111842 springs and¦111822 the lower¦111845 springs.
\s1 The victory of descendants of Yehudah and Benyamin
\rem /s1 The Victories of the Tribes of Judah and Benjamin
\p
\v 16 The Kenites¦111849 (descendants of Mosheh's¦111851 father-in-law¦111850), left the city¦111853 of palm¦111854 trees¦111854 \add !(Yeriho/Jericho)\add* and went with Yehudah's¦111858 descendants to the southern wilderness¦111859 near Arad¦111863 and lived¦111865 with the people¦111868 \add there\add*.
\v 17 Then¦111870 the warriors of Yehudah¦111871 and Simeon¦111874 conquered the Canaanites¦111879 living¦111880 at Tsefat¦111881 and destroyed¦111882 \add @the city¦111889\add*. \add Now\add* it's called¦111884 Hormah¦111890 \add (which means ‘complete destruction’)\add*.
\v 18 \add *They¦111893\add* also captured¦111892 Gaza¦111896, Ashkelon¦111902, and Ekron¦111908 and \add all\add* their surrounding territories.
\rem /s1 Israel Fails to Conquer the Land
\v 19 Yahweh¦111914 helped Yehudah¦111917 and¦111921 \add #they\add* took¦111921 possession¦111921 of¦111921 the hill¦111924 country¦111924, although they weren't able to¦111927 conquer the people living down in the plains¦111931 because¦111925 they had iron¦111935 on their chariots¦111934.
\v 20 They gave¦111938 Hebron¦111942 to¦111939 Caleb¦111939 just¦111943 as¦111943 Mosheh¦111945 had¦111944 directed, and¦111938 he dispossessed¦111946 the three¦111950 sons¦111951 of¦111951 the Anak¦111952 from there¦111947.\x + \xo 1:20: \xt Josh 15:13-14.\x*
\v 21 \add ≈However\add* Benyamin's¦111962 descendants¦111961 didn't¦111959 drive the Yebusites¦111956 living¦111957 in Yerushalem out, so \add *they¦111960\add* have lived¦111963 among the Benyamites in Yerushalem to this¦111972 day¦111971.\x + \xo 1:21: \xt Josh 15:63; 2Sam 5:6; 1Ch 11:4.\x*
\s1 Efrayim and Menashsheh conquer Beyt-El
\rem /s1 The Tribes of Ephraim and Manasseh Conquer Beyt-El
\p
\v 22 Also¦111982 Yosef's¦111978 descendants attacked Beyt-El, and Yahweh¦111985 \add ≈helped\add* them¦111986.
\v 23 \add *They¦111988\add* sent spies out to Beyt-El¦111991 (previously called Luz¦111999)
\v 24 and \add *they¦112001\add* saw¦112001 a man¦112003 coming¦112004 out¦112004 of the city and told him¦112009, “Please¦112012 show¦112010 us¦112010 an entrance¦112014 into the city, and we'll¦112016 be kind to you¦112017.”
\v 25 \add ≈So¦112020\add* he¦112020 showed¦112020 them¦112020 an entrance¦112023, and \add @their warriors\add* \add attacked and\add* \add ≈killed the inhabitants\add*, but¦112020 they let¦112040 the man¦112034 and his¦112039 family¦112039 go¦112040 free.
\v 26 The¦112043 man¦112043 went¦112042 to the land¦112044 of¦112044 the Hittites¦112045 and \add ≈founded\add* a city¦112047 and called it Luz¦112050 (which it's still called to this¦112055 day¦112054).
\s1 The locals not driven out by the Israelis
\rem /s1 People Who Were Not Driven Out by the Israelites
\p
\v 27 \add ≈However\add* Menashsheh's¦112061 \add descendants\add* didn't¦112058 drive out those living in Beyt¦112064-Shan¦112066, Taanak¦112072, Dor¦112080, Yibleam, or Meggidon, because the Canaanites¦112099 were determined¦112098 \add ≈to¦112100 keep living there\add*.\x + \xo 1:27-28: \xt Josh 17:11-13.\x*
\v 28 (\add Later,\add* the Israelis¦112108 became¦112107 stronger¦112107 and¦112104 \add ≈they forced¦112113 the Canaanites¦112112 to work¦112113 for them as their slaves\add*, but¦112104 they didn't¦112115 drive¦112114 them out.)
\v 29 Nor did Efrayim's¦112119 \add descendants\add* drive the Canaanites¦112124 out of¦112129 Gezer¦112126, so \add *they\add* continued to live among them.\x + \xo 1:29: \xt Josh 16:10.\x*
\p
\v 30 Zebulun's¦112133 \add descendants\add* didn't¦112134 drive the Canaanites¦112145 out of Kitron¦112139 or Nahalol¦112143, so they¦112147 continued to live among them and were¦112147 \add ≈forced¦112148 to work¦112148 as slaves\add*.
\p
\v 31 Asher's¦112151 \add descendants\add* didn't¦112152 drive out those who were living in Akko¦112157, Tsidon¦112161, Ahlab¦112164, Aczib¦112167, Helbah¦112170, Afek¦112173, or Rehob¦112176,
\v 32 \add ≈So¦112178\add* the Asherites¦112179 lived with¦112180 the Canaanites¦112181 who \add still\add* lived in¦112180 the region because¦112184 they didn't¦112185 drive¦112186 them out.
\p
\v 33 Naftali's¦112189 \add descendants\add* didn't¦112190 drive out those who were living in¦112206 Beyt-Shemesh¦112198 or Beyt-Anat¦112204, so they¦112216 continued to live among them and were \add ≈forced¦112218 to work¦112218 as slaves\add*.
\p
\v 34 The Amorites¦112222 forced Dan's¦112227 descendants¦112225 to live in the hill¦112228 country¦112228, because¦112229 they¦112221 didn't¦112231 allow¦112232 them to come¦112233 down¦112233 to the plains¦112234.
\v 35 The¦112237 Amorites were determined¦112236 to live¦112238 at¦112239 Mt¦112239. Heres¦112241, in¦112239 Ayyalon¦112242 and¦112236 Shaalbim¦112243 (but¦112236 when Yosef's¦112248 descendants became stronger, they¦112249 forced¦112250 the Amorites \add ≈to work¦112250 as slaves\add*).
\v 36 The Amorites¦112253 border¦112252 went from the¦112254 Scorpion¦112255 Pass through Sela¦112256 and¦112257 upward¦112257 from there.
\c 2
\s1 God's messenger comes to Bokim
\rem /s1 The Angel of the Lord at Bochim; Result of disobedience; Israel's Disobedience; The Angel of the Lord at Bokim; The Lord's Messenger Comes to Bokim
\p
\v 1 Then Yahweh's¦112263 messenger¦112261 went from¦112264 Gilgal¦112266 to Bokim¦112269 and told \add the Israelis\add*, “I brought¦112272 you all out of¦112282 Egypt¦112274 and led you here to this land¦112279 that I promised to your ancestors¦112282. I had said¦112283 that I wouldn't ever break¦112286 my¦112287 agreement¦112287 with¦112288 you all,
\v 2 but¦112291 that¦112298 you mustn't form treaties with¦112304 the peoples who lived here, rather you must¦112300 tear down¦112300 their¦112299 altars¦112299. But¦112291 you all haven't obeyed what I said. What have¦112308 you \add gone and\add* done¦112308?\x + \xo 2:2: \xt Exo 34:12-13; Deu 7:2-5.\x*
\v 3 \add ≈So¦112310\add* I'm telling you that I won't¦112312 drive¦112314 your enemies out¦112314 ahead¦112316 of¦112316 you, \add ≈but¦112310\add* they'll¦112317 be¦112317 like thorns in your sides¦112319 and¦112310 you'll all end up being enslaved to their¦112320 \add ≈idols\add*.”
\v 4 When¦112325 Yahweh's¦112328 messenger¦112327 finished saying that, the people wept¦112344 loudly,
\v 5 so they called¦112346 that¦112350 place¦112349 Bokim¦112351 \add (which means ‘weeping’)\add* and¦112352 they \add ≈offered sacrifices¦112352\add* to¦112355 Yahweh¦112355 there¦112354.
\s1 The death of Yehoshua
\rem /s1 Death of Joshua; Disobedience and Defeat; The Death of Joshua
\p
\v 6 Then¦112366 Yehoshua¦112359 dismissed¦112358 the people¦112362 and¦112363 the \add ≈tribes went¦112363 to their various regions\add* to take¦112369 possession¦112369 of¦112369 the land¦112372.
\v 7 The people¦112375 \add ≈obeyed\add* Yahweh \add ≈as long¦112387 as Yehoshua¦112381 and¦112382 the elders¦112385 who had seen¦112392 the great¦112398 things that¦112399 Yahweh had done¦112400 for¦112401 Yisrael¦112401, were alive\add*.
\v 8 Then¦112403 Yahweh's¦112409 servant¦112408 Yehoshua¦112404 (son¦112405 of Nun¦112407) died¦112403 at the¦112408 age of 110
\v 9 and¦112416 they buried¦112416 him¦112417 in¦112418 his allocated land at¦112418 Timnat¦112420-Heres, inside Efrayim's¦112424 hill¦112426 country¦112423 territory¦112418 north¦112425 of Mt¦112426. Gaash¦112428.\x + \xo 2:9: \xt Josh 19:49-50.\x*
\s1 Yisrael (Israel) turns from Yahweh
\rem /s1 Israel Disobeys the Lord
\p
\v 10 When¦112430 all¦112431 of¦112431 that¦112443 generation \add ≈had¦112455 passed away\add*, the next generation didn't¦112444 know Yahweh¦112449 or \add even\add* know about¦112431 what he'd done¦112455 for \add ≈the Israelis¦112456\add*.
\rem /s1 Israel's Unfaithfulness; Israel Stops Worshipping the Lord
\v 11 \add *They¦112462\add* did \add ≈things that Yahweh¦112467 considered evil¦112465\add* and worshipped Baal¦112471 \add idols\add*
\v 12 and abandoned¦112473 the god¦112478 of their¦112479 ancestors¦112479, Yahweh, the one who¦112480 brought¦112480 them out of Egypt¦112483. \add Instead\add* they¦112484 went¦112484 after¦112485 other¦112488 gods—the gods of the people¦112490 around¦112492 them. They¦112493 worshipped them and that¦112491 made Yahweh angry.
\v 13 They¦112502 abandoned¦112502 Yahweh¦112505 and \add ≈worshipped\add* the Baal¦112507 and Ashtarot¦112508 \add idols\add*.
\v 14 \add ≈So¦112510\add* Yahweh¦112513 \add ≈got angry with¦112514\add* Yisrael¦112514 so \add ≈he caused raiders to come and take their property\add* and \add ≈he allowed their enemies¦112523 to become more powerful\add* so they could no longer stand¦112529 against them.
\v 15 \add ≈Whenever the¦112537 Israelis went¦112536 into battle\add*, Yahweh¦112539 \add ≈worked against them¦112542\add* just¦112544 like \add *he'd\add* said he would and they¦112536 became very distressed¦112551.
\rem /s1 The Lord Rescues His People; The first judges
\p
\v 16 Then¦112555 Yahweh¦112556 \add ≈gave them\add* leaders\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Traditionally called ‘judges’ in most English translations, but that modern term doesn't fit their various functions in this account at all well. (‘Leaders’ isn't perfect either but it's generic enough to cover most of their roles.)\f* to save them from¦112559 the¦112559 raiders.
\v 17 \add ≈However\add* they wouldn't listen¦112567 to \add those\add* leaders \add either\add*—continuing to prostitute¦112569 themselves to other¦112572 gods¦112571 and bowing¦112573 down¦112573 to idols. They¦112575 rapidly turned¦112575 away from¦112577 their¦112582 ancestors' ways¦112579 and \add ≈showed no interest\add* in obeying¦112583 Yahweh's¦112586 instructions.
\v 18 Whenever Yahweh¦112595 \add ≈gave them\add* a leader, he would \add ≈support\add* that leader and would save them from their¦112617 enemies¦112606 during that leader's life, because¦112592 he pitied \add @the people\add* when they groaned¦112614 \add ≈from being tormented and oppressed¦112616\add*.
\v 19 \add ≈But¦112619 then¦112619 at¦112621 the death of that leader\add*, they¦112623 would turn back and behave more¦112625 corruptly¦112624 than¦112625 their ancestors¦112625—following other¦112629 gods¦112628 and bowing¦112631 down¦112631 to¦112632 \add @idols\add*. They¦112634 didn't¦112633 stop any¦112635 of their evil activities or their shameless behaviour
\v 20 \add ≈so\add* Yahweh¦112642 \add ≈was very angry\add* at¦112643 Yisrael¦112643 and said¦112644, “This nation¦112648 has broken the agreement¦112652 that¦112653 I made with¦112643 their¦112657 ancestors¦112657 and haven't \add ≈done what I told¦112654 them to¦112660\add*.
\v 21 I¦112664 won't¦112665 keep driving the¦112672 people groups away that¦112673 \add remained\add* when Yehoshua¦112676 died¦112677,
\v 22 so as¦112679 to use them to test¦112680 Yisrael¦112684 \add to see\add* whether¦112685 \add @the Israelis¦112690\add* will¦112686 obey my instructions like their¦112695 ancestors¦112695 did, or¦112696 not¦112698.
\v 23 \add ≈So¦112700\add* Yahweh¦112701 allowed those¦112705 people groups to stay on¦112711, and¦112700 hence they hadn't been \add ≈handed over\add* to Yehoshua¦112713.
\c 3
\s1 The remaining kingdoms in Canaan
\rem /s1 Nations Remaining in the Land; The Nations Left in Canaan; The Nations Remaining in the Land
\p
\v 1 The \add following\add* are the kingdoms¦112717 which¦112718 Yahweh¦112720 allowed to remain so as to test¦112721 the \add newer generations\add* of¦112727 Yisrael¦112725 who¦112729 hadn't experienced the battles¦112736 in¦112722 Canaan¦112737
\v 2 (\add if\add* only¦112748 for the sake of teaching¦112746 warfare to¦112746 those generations¦112742 who hadn't experienced¦112753 it¦112753):
\v 3 the five¦112755 Philistine rulers¦112757 and the Canaanites¦112761, the Sidonians¦112762, the Hivites¦112763 that live on Mt. Lebanon¦112766 between Mt. Baal¦112769-Hermon and Lebo¦112771-Hamat¦112772.\f + \fr 3:3 \ft Or, ‘the entrance to Hamat’.\f*
\v 4 They¦112774 were¦112774 \add left\add* to test¦112775 Yisrael¦112779 to find out if they¦112781 would obey¦112781 Yahweh's¦112785 instructions¦112788 that¦112786 had¦112788 been given through Mosheh¦112794 to their¦112791 ancestors¦112791,
\v 5 and \add so\add* the Israelis¦112797 lived among the Canaanites¦112800, the Hittites¦112801, the Amorites¦112802, the Perizzites¦112803, the Hivites¦112804, and the Yebusites¦112805.
\v 6 They¦112807 took¦112807 their daughters as wives¦112812, and gave¦112816 their own daughters \add to be married\add* to their sons¦112817, and they¦112818 \add ≈worshipped\add* their gods¦112821.
\s1 Otniel
\rem /s1 Othniel; Othniel Becomes Israel's Judge
\p
\v 7 \add ≈So\add* the \add ≈Israelis¦112827 did things considered evil¦112830 by\add* Yahweh and they¦112824 forgot¦112833 about¦112825 their¦112837 god¦112837 Yahweh, \add ≈instead\add* serving¦112838 the Baal¦112841 and Asherot \add idols\add*.
\v 8 \add Because of that,\add* Yahweh¦112849 \add ≈was very angry\add* with¦112850 Yisrael¦112850, so he¦112851 \add ≈handed them¦112851 over to\add* King¦112855 Cushan¦112864-Rishathaim¦112854 of Aram¦112857-Naharaim \add !(Mesopotamia¦112857)\add*. The Israelis¦112861 served¦112858 him for eight¦112866 years¦112867
\v 9 \add ≈but¦112869 when\add* they¦112869 cried¦112869 out¦112869 to Yahweh¦112875, \add *he\add* \add ≈sent them\add* a \add ≈leader to rescue\add* them: Otniel¦112883, son¦112884 of Caleb's¦112888 younger brother¦112887 Kenaz¦112886.
\v 10 Yahweh's¦112896 spirit¦112894 \add ≈empowered\add* him and he led Yisrael¦112900. He \add ≈led them into battle¦112902\add* against King¦112910 Cushan¦112908-Rishathaim¦112909 of¦112910 Aram¦112911, and Yahweh \add ≈helped them\add* defeat \add *him\add*.
\v 11 \add ≈So¦112918\add* the region had peace¦112918 for forty¦112920 years¦112921, and¦112918 then¦112918 Otniel¦112923 (Kenaz's¦112926 son¦112924) died¦112922.
\s1 Ehud
\rem /s1 Ehud; Ehud Becomes Israel's Judge
\p
\v 12 \add Once\add* again the Israelis¦112931 started doing¦112932 things considered evil¦112933 by Yahweh¦112935, \add ≈so\add* because¦112948 of that, \add *he¦112936\add* strengthened¦112936 King¦112941 Eglon¦112940 of Moab¦112943 against Yisrael¦112931.
\v 13 \add @King Eglon\add* \add ≈enlisted warriors from\add* the Ammonites¦112962 and the Amalekites¦112963, and they¦112969 attacked Yisrael¦112968 and captured \add Yeriho/Jericho,\add* the city¦112972 of¦112972 palm¦112973 trees¦112973,
\v 14 \add ≈so\add* the Israelis¦112978 served¦112975 \add *him\add* for eighteen¦112985 years¦112987.
\p
\v 15 \add ≈But¦112990\add* the Israelis cried¦112990 out¦112990 to Yahweh¦112996, \add *he¦112997\add* \add ≈sent them\add* a \add ≈leader to rescue\add* them: the left-hander Ehud¦113003, son¦113004 of Gera¦113006 the Benyamite. The Israelis sent him to carry the tribute¦113020 payment to King¦113022 Eglon¦113021 of Moab¦113023.
\v 16 \add ≈Now\add* Ehud¦113027 had made¦113025 himself a double-edged sword¦113028—a short one about¦113030 half a metre long—and¦113025 he strapped it to his right¦113040 thigh¦113039 beneath his clothes¦113037.
\v 17 Then¦113042 he¦113042 presented¦113042 the¦113045 tribute¦113045 to¦113046 King¦113049 Eglon¦113046 of¦113047 Moab¦113048, who was¦113050 a very¦113052 fat¦113051 man¦113050,
\v 18 and¦113054 after leaving \add for home\add*, he sent¦113061 off the people¦113064 who'd \add actually\add* carried¦113065 it \add all\add*.
\v 19 Then¦113068 from¦113070 the stone quarries \add that they'd reached\add* near Gilgal¦113076, he returned¦113069 and¦113068 told the king that he had a private message¦113078 for him¦113087. The king told his attendants¦113090 to be quiet then¦113068 sent them out¦113086 of¦113088 the room.
\p
\v 20 \add ≈So¦113093\add* Ehud¦113107 approached where the king was¦113099 sitting¦113099 alone¦113105 in¦113100 the coolness¦113101 of¦113100 his upper room and¦113093 told him¦113104, “I have a message¦113108 for¦113105 you¦113112 from God¦113110.” \add @The king\add* stood up from \add &his\add* seat¦113115
