\p
\v 25 “We¦116187 \add ≈can certainly do that for you\add*,” they \add ≈replied¦116183\add*. So¦116183 they spread¦116186 out¦116186 a garment¦116189 and¦116183 each man threw¦116190 the \add #earrings¦116193\add* from his spoil onto it.
\v 26 Now the weight¦116197 of the gold earrings¦116198 of gold came to twenty kilograms, and¦116196 then¦116196 there were the crescent-shaped ornaments¦116210, the pendants¦116211, the dyed purple¦116213 garments¦116212 that the Midianite kings¦116215 had been wearing, as well as the collars that had been around their¦116223 camels' necks¦116222.
\v 27 Then¦116225 Gideon made¦116225 \add @the gold\add* into¦116228 an¦116228 idol\f + \fr 8:27 \ft We don't really know what ‘efod’ means in this context. (It was also used to refer to priestly clothing.)\f* and placed¦116229 it in¦116231 his \add home\add* city¦116231 of Ophrah¦116232, and the Israelis¦116234 prostituted¦116233 themselves to it there¦116238, and so it became like a trap for¦116240 Gideon and his household¦116241.
