\rem /s1 Abimelech; Abimelech and his successors; Abimelech Rules over Shechem; Abimelech Attempts to Establish a Monarchy
\p
\v 1 \add ≈Now\add* Gideon's son¦116367 Abimelek¦116366 went¦116365 to Shekem¦116370 to his mother's brothers¦116373 and spoke¦116375 to \add @those uncles\add* and to all¦116379 the \add ≈extended\add* family of his mother's father¦116384, saying¦116386,
\v 2 “Please¦116390 \add ≈ask around\add* all¦116392 the¦116394 citizens¦116394 of¦116394 Shekem¦116395: ‘What¦116396 is¦116398 better for¦116399 you: Gideon's seventy¦116402 sons¦116405 ruling over¦116401 you, or¦116407 having a single¦116412 man¦116411 \add ≈in¦116391 charge\add*?’ Remember¦116413 that¦116414 I'm your flesh¦116421 and¦116421 bone¦116416.”
\v 3 \add ≈So his uncles working\add* on¦116430 his behalf, passed that onto all the citizens¦116433 of Shekem¦116434, and¦116440 \add ≈they became convinced in¦116430\add* Abimelek¦116443, because¦116444 they said¦116445, “He's¦116447 our¦116446 brother¦116446.”
\v 4 Then¦116449 they¦116449 gave¦116449 him seventy¦116452 silver¦116453 coins from¦116454 the¦116454 temple¦116454 of Baal¦116458-Berith¦116462, and¦116449 Abimelek¦116465 \add ≈used them¦116464 to entice some rough lay-abouts to work for\add* him.
\v 5 Then¦116472 \add #they\add* went¦116472 to his father's¦116475 house¦116473 at Ofrah¦116476, and¦116472 he killed¦116477 his seventy¦116484 \add half-\add*brothers (Gideon's sons¦116481) there on¦116489 one¦116492 stone¦116491. But¦116472 Gideon's youngest son¦116495 Yotam¦116494 survived¦116493 because¦116499 he had hidden¦116500 \add from them\add*.
\v 6 Then all of the \add prominent\add* citizens¦116506 of Shekem and all of Beyt¦116510-Millo¦116511 assembled themselves, and they¦116512 went¦116512 and installed Abimelek¦116516 as king¦116517 beside an oak tree¦116520 near¦116518 the standing pillar\f + \fr 9:6 \ft Likely connected with idol worship.\f* that¦116521 was¦116523 in¦116523 Shekem.
\s1 Yotam's tree parable
\rem /s1 Jotham's Parable; The Parable of the Trees
\p
\v 7 \add ≈When¦116525\add* they told¦116525 Yotam¦116526, he¦116527 went¦116527 and stood¦116528 on¦116529 the top¦116529 of Mt¦116530. Gerizim¦116532 and shouted out to them¦116537 all \add ≈in¦116529 a loud voice¦116534\add*, “Listen¦116538 to me¦116539, citizens¦116540 of Shekem¦116541, so¦116542 God¦116544 will listen¦116542 to all of you.
\v 8 Once the trees¦116548 went¦116547 about in order to appoint a king¦116551 for themselves¦116550, \add ≈So\add* they¦116552 said¦116552 to the \em olive¦116553 tree¦116553\em*, ‘Reign¦116554 over¦116556 us¦116556.’
\v 9 \add ≈But¦116558\add* the olive¦116560 tree¦116560 replied¦116558 to them¦116559, ‘Have I stopped producing my¦116564 \add ≈valuable oil¦116564 used to\add* honour¦116568 gods¦116569 and¦116558 men¦116570, that¦116565 I should go¦116571 to sway¦116572 above the \add other\add* trees¦116575?’
\v 10 \add ≈So¦116577\add* the trees¦116578 said¦116577 to¦116579 the \em fig¦116579 tree¦116579\em*, ‘You¦116582 come¦116580 \add and\add* reign¦116583 over¦116584 us¦116584.’
\v 11 \add ≈But¦116586\add* the fig¦116588 tree¦116588 \add answered\add* them, ‘Have I stopped producing my sweetness¦116592 and¦116586 my good¦116596 fruit¦116595, that I should go¦116597 to¦116598 sway¦116598 above the \add other\add* trees¦116601?’
\v 12 Then¦116603 the trees¦116604 said¦116603 to¦116605 the grapevine¦116605, ‘You¦116608 come¦116606 \add and\add* reign¦116609 over¦116611 us¦116611.’
\v 13 \add ≈But¦116613\add* the vine¦116615 \add ≈replied¦116613\add*, ‘Have I stopped producing my¦116619 new¦116619 wine¦116619, which¦116620 cheers¦116620 gods¦116621 and¦116613 man¦116622, that¦116620 I should go¦116623 to¦116624 sway¦116624 over¦116625 the \add other\add* trees¦116627?’
\v 14 Then¦116629 all¦116630 the trees¦116632 said¦116629 to¦116633 the thornbush¦116635, ‘\em You\em* come¦116636 \add and\add* reign¦116638 over¦116640 us¦116640!’
\v 15 The¦116643 thornbush told the trees¦116646, ‘If¦116647 you all are¦116650 \add ≈really wanted to anoint¦116650\add* me¦116651 as king¦116652 over¦116653 you, come¦116654 \add and\add* take¦116655 refuge¦116655 in¦116648 \em my\em* shade¦116656. But¦116642 if¦116657 \add ≈you all don't¦116659\add*, may the thornbush send out fire¦116661 to \add ≈burn up\add* Lebanon's¦116669 cedar¦116668 \add forests\add*.’
\p
\v 16 “\add ≈Therefore did\add* you all act¦116676 with¦116674 honesty and integrity¦116675 when you installed Abimelek¦116680 as king¦116677? Did you all treat \add ≈Gideon's family\add* well, and did you all \add ≈repay him¦116696 appropriately for what he did for you all\add*?
\v 17 My¦116701 father¦116701 fought¦116700 on¦116702 your behalf, and¦116703 even \add ≈risked¦116703\add* his¦116706 life¦116706 out front¦116707 when he¦116708 \add ≈rescued you all from¦116710 the¦116710 Midianites¦116711\add*,
\v 18 \add ≈but¦116713\add* today you've all risen¦116714 up¦116714 against my father's¦116718 family and¦116713 killed¦116720 his sons¦116723—seventy¦116724 men¦116725 on¦116726 one¦116729 stone¦116728. Then¦116713 you've installed Abimelek¦116733, the son¦116734 of his female¦116736 servant, as king¦116730 over¦116737 the citizens¦116739 of Shekem¦116740 because¦116741 he's¦116743 your¦116742 brother¦116742.
\v 19 \add ≈Now\add* if¦116745 you've all acted¦116749 with honesty and integrity¦116748 with towards \add ≈Gideon¦116752 and his family\add* today, be happy with Abimelek¦116759, and he'll also¦116761 be happy with all of you,
\v 20 \add ≈but¦116766\add* if¦116766 not¦116768, then¦116766 fire¦116770 will come out of Abimelek¦116771 and destroy the citizens of Shekem and Beyt-Millo. Fire¦116782 will also come out from the Shekem's¦116776 citizens and from Beyt-Millo, and it'll destroy Abimelech¦116790.”
\v 21 Then¦116792 Yotam¦116793 fled¦116792 away and escaped and went¦116795 to¦116796 Be'er¦116796 and settled there¦116798 away from¦116799 the presence of¦116799 his¦116801 \add half-\add*brother Abimelek¦116800.
\rem /s1 Shechem's Revolt against Abimelech; The Downfall of Abimelech
\p
\v 22 Abimelek¦116805 ruled¦116804 over¦116806 Yisrael¦116808 for three¦116809 years¦116810,
\v 23 then¦116812 God¦116813 sent¦116812 an evil¦116815 spirit¦116814 between¦116816 \add *him¦116817\add* and¦116812 the citizens of¦116819 Shekem¦116820, \add ≈so\add* the citizens \add *there\add* dealt¦116821 treacherously¦116821 with¦116825 Abimelek¦116817.
\v 24 The violent¦116828 treatment of \add ≈Gideon's\add* seventy¦116829 sons¦116830 \add ≈kept getting brought up\add*, and by \add ≈it was all blamed on\add* their¦116838 brother¦116838 Abimelech¦116837 who had killed¦116840 them¦116841, and on \add those of\add* Shekem's¦116844 citizens¦116843 who \add ≈assisted him in\add* the killing of his brothers¦116854.
\v 25 \add ≈So¦116856\add* the Shekem¦116859 citizens¦116858 placed men in¦116872 ambush¦116860 along the tops¦116862 of¦116862 the hills¦116863 \add ≈to get him\add*, \add ≈but¦116856\add* they¦116864 robbed¦116864 everyone¦116866 who¦116868 went passed¦116870 them¦116871 along the road¦116872, and¦116856 \add so\add* it¦116873 was¦116873 reported¦116873 to Abimelech¦116865.
\p
\v 26 Then¦116889 Gaal¦116878 (Ebed's¦116881 son¦116879) came¦116877 along with¦116884 his¦116882 brothers¦116882 and \add ≈moved into\add* Shekem¦116884, and the¦116888 citizens¦116888 \add *there\add* \add began to\add* trust¦116885 \add #them\add*.
\v 27 They went out¦116891 into the field¦116892, and they picked grapes¦116893 from their vineyards¦116896 and pressed them¦116897. They celebrated harvest festivals and they went into the house¦116901 of their gods¦116902, then¦116891 they ate¦116903 and drank¦116904, and they cursed¦116905 Abimelek¦116908.
\v 28 Then¦116910 Gaal¦116912 (Ebed's¦116915 son) said¦116910, “Who's¦116916 Abimelek, and who's¦116919 Shekem, that we should serve him¦116923? Isn't \add @Abimelek\add* \add ≈Gideon's\add* son, and Zebul¦116928 is¦116928 his¦116929 deputy¦116929? Serve¦116930 the descendants of Shekem's¦116921 father¦116935 Hamor¦116934, \add ≈but¦116910\add* why¦116937 should we serve \add @Abimelek\add*?
\v 29 Who¦116941 will \add ≈fight with¦116947 me to defeat those\add* people¦116945 and depose Abimelek? Then¦116941 he said¦116952 to Abimelek, ‘\add ≈Gather\add* your¦116955 army¦116955 and come¦116956 \add ≈and fight\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 30 When¦116958 Zebul¦116959, the prince of the city¦116962, heard \add ≈what Ga'al¦116966 (Ebed's¦116969 son¦116967) had said\add*, \add ≈he got very angry\add*
\v 31 and he¦116973 sent¦116973 messengers¦116974 to Abimelek¦116977 in¦116978 secret, saying¦116979, “Listen¦116980, Ebed's¦116984 son¦116982 Gaal¦116981 and his¦116985 brothers¦116985 are¦116986 coming¦116986 to Shekem¦116987, and wow¦116988, they're¦116988 inciting the¦116992 city¦116992 against¦116993 you¦116993.
\v 32 You¦116998 and the people¦116999 who are with¦117004 you¦117002 \add ≈should go out at¦117004 night¦116997\add* and lie¦117003 in ambush in the countryside¦117004.
\v 33 Then¦117006 \add ≈when the sun¦117009 rises¦117008\add* in the morning¦117007, you all should set out early¦117010 and spread out in an attack against¦117023 the city¦117014. Keep¦117015 watch, then when \add @Ga'al\add* and the people¦117018 with¦117007 him are¦117022 coming out toward you \add to fight\add*, then you \add can overpower them and\add* \add ≈treat him however you think fit\add*.”
\p
\v 34 \add ≈So\add* Abimelek¦117032 and all¦117033 the people¦117035 with¦117038 him¦117038 \add ≈went out in the night¦117039\add* and they¦117040 set up four¦117044 units in an ambush against Shekem¦117043.
\v 35 \add In the morning,\add* Ga'al (Ebed's¦117051 son¦117049) went¦117047 out and he¦117052 stood at the entrance¦117053 of the city¦117055 gate¦117054. Then¦117047 Abimelek¦117057 and \add ≈his men stood up¦117056 from¦117062 their hiding places\add*.
\v 36 \add ≈When¦117066\add* Ga'al saw¦117066 the people, he¦117072 said¦117072 to¦117073 Zebul¦117075, “Look, a group of people is¦117079 coming¦117079 down¦117079 from¦117080 the tops¦117080 of the hills!”
\p “You are seeing¦117089 the shadow¦117086 of¦117080 the hills as men¦117090,” Zebul \add ≈responded¦117072\add*.
\p
\v 37 \add ≈But¦117093\add* Ga'al said¦117097 again¦117094, “Look, groups of people¦117100 are¦117101 coming down¦117101 from beside the \add ≈top of the hill\add*, and¦117105 \add ≈another group\add* is¦117108 coming from the way of the ‘oak of the diviners’.”
\p
\v 38 Then¦117113 Zebul¦117115 said to¦117114 him, “\add ≈Well, you were the one who\add* said, ‘Who¦117121 is¦117122 Abimelek¦117122 that¦117128 we¦117124 should serve¦117124 him?’ Aren't these the people¦117127 who you \add ≈were scorning\add*? Indeed now¦117134, go¦117131 out¦117131 and¦117113 fight¦117135 against \add #them\add*.”
\v 39 \add ≈So\add* Ga'al went¦117139 out¦117139 in¦117145 front¦117141 of¦117142 Shekem's¦117143 citizens¦117142 and¦117144 fought¦117144 against¦117145 Abimelek¦117145.
\v 40 Abimelek¦117148 pursued¦117147 him, and¦117147 he¦117149 \add ≈had to flee¦117149 away from¦117150 him\add*. Many¦117153 \add of their men\add* fell¦117151 fatally¦117152 wounded¦117152 as far as the entrance¦117156 of the city gate¦117157.
\v 41 Then¦117159 Abimelek¦117160 settled in Arumah¦117161, \add ≈but¦117159\add* Zebul¦117163 drove¦117162 Ga'al¦117166 and¦117159 his¦117169 brothers¦117169 away from¦117170 living¦117170 in Shekem¦117171.
\p
\v 42 Now it so happened that the next¦117174 day¦117174, the people¦117176 \add of Shekem\add* went¦117175 out into the countryside¦117177, and¦117173 \add ≈it was reported¦117178\add* to¦117179 Abimelek¦117179.
\v 43 So¦117181 he took¦117181 \add ≈his men\add* and split them into¦117186 three¦117186 units. Then¦117181 he waited¦117188 in ambush in the countryside¦117189. When¦117182 he saw¦117190 and wow, the people¦117192 were going¦117193 out¦117193 of the city¦117196, then¦117181 he \add ≈attacked¦117199\add* and killed¦117199 them.
\v 44 Abimelek¦117201 and the units that¦117203 were with¦117204 him¦117204 had spread out and they¦117206 stood¦117206 at the entrance¦117207 of the city¦117209 gate¦117208, and two¦117210 units spread out against everyone¦117215 who¦117217 was in¦117218 the field¦117218 and killed¦117219 them¦117219.
\v 45 Abimelek¦117221 \add ≈spent that¦117234 whole¦117224 day¦117225 attacking Shekem\add*. He¦117227 captured¦117227 the city¦117230 and \add #they\add* killed¦117237 the people¦117233 who were in¦117223 it. Then¦117221 he tore \add *it\add* down, and scattered salt¦117243 on¦117223 \add @the land\add*.
\p
\v 46 When all¦117247 the lords of the tower¦117250 of Shekem¦117252 heard¦117246, they¦117253 went¦117253 into¦117254 the citadel of the temple for El¦117258-Berit¦117259,
\v 47 \add ≈but¦117261\add* it¦117261 was¦117261 reported to¦117262 Abimelek¦117262 that¦117263 all¦117265 the lords of¦117267 the Shekem¦117270 tower¦117268 had¦117264 gathered¦117264 there.
\v 48 So¦117272 Abimelek¦117273 and all the people with him, went up to Mt¦117274. Tsalmon¦117276. \add *He¦117277\add* took¦117284 axes¦117288 and cut¦117290 a branch¦117291 down from the trees, then¦117272 lifted¦117293 it onto his¦117297 shoulder¦117297 and said¦117298 to the people who were with him, “\add ≈What¦117285 I¦117307 just did, you all quickly do¦117309 the same\add*.”
\v 49 So¦117312 all the people also each¦117315 cut¦117312 off a branch¦117319 and then¦117312 \add ≈followed¦117320\add* Abimelek¦117322. They piled them¦117323 on¦117332 top of the underground chamber, \add ≈and that's how\add* they¦117327 burnt out the underground chamber. As a result, all the distinguished people in¦117332 the tower¦117338 of Shekem¦117340 also died¦117333—about¦117341 a thousand¦117341 men¦117337 and women¦117343.
\p
\v 50 Then Abimelek¦117347 went¦117346 to¦117348 Tebets and set up camp around \add *the city\add* and captured¦117353 it¦117353.
\v 51 \add ≈But¦117355\add* there¦117363 was a strong tower in the middle¦117359 of the city¦117361, so all of the men¦117366 and the women¦117367 and all the prominent citizens¦117369 fled¦117362 to it. \add ≈Once they were all in, they barricaded it up\add* and they went¦117373 up onto the tower roof¦117376.
\v 52 Then¦117379 Abimelek¦117380 \add and his men\add* approached¦117386 the tower and fought¦117384 against it, and he¦117386 came¦117379 in as close¦117386 as the tower entrance¦117389 in order to \add ≈set fire¦117392 to it\add*,
\v 53 \add ≈but¦117394\add* a woman¦117395 threw¦117394 an¦117398 upper¦117398 millstone¦117398 down onto Abimelek's¦117404 head¦117401 and¦117403 it crushed¦117403 his¦117406 skull¦117406.\x + \xo 9:53: \xt 2Sam 11:21.\x*
\v 54 Then¦117408 he cried out hurriedly to the¦117412 young man bearing his armour¦117415, “Draw¦117418 out your¦117419 sword¦117419 and¦117408 put me to death, \add ≈because I don't want it¦117417 to be said¦117416 afterwards that I was killed¦117426 by\add* a woman¦117425.” \add ≈So¦117408\add* his young man stabbed him so that he died¦117429.
\v 55 \add ≈When¦117431\add* the¦117432 \add ≈Israeli¦117434 warriors\add* saw¦117431 that¦117435 Abimelek¦117437 was¦117436 dead¦117436, they¦117431 all returned to¦117440 their own¦117440 homes.
\p
\v 56 Thus God¦117443 avenged the¦117445 evil¦117445 of¦117445 Abimelek¦117446 that¦117447 he¦117448 had¦117448 done¦117448 to¦117449 his father¦117449 \add Gideon\add* by¦117450 killing¦117450 his seventy¦117453 brothers¦117454,
\v 57 and God¦117463 \add ≈caused all¦117457 the evil¦117459 done by Shekem's¦117461 men¦117460\add* to return back onto their¦117464 own heads¦117464, and the curse¦117467 of \add ≈Gideon's¦117471\add* \add youngest\add* son¦117469 Yotam¦117468, came¦117465 true on¦117464 them.
\c 10
\s1 Tola
