\v 6 Then¦118989 the¦118990 woman¦118990 went¦118989 and explained to her¦118992 husband¦118992, “A man¦118994 of God came¦118996 to me. \add ≈He looked like one of God's messengers¦119000\add*—\add ≈extremely terrifying\add*—\add ≈so\add* I¦119005 didn't \add like to\add* ask¦119005 him¦119005 \add ≈where¦119006 he'd come from¦119008\add*, and he didn't \add ≈tell¦119015\add* me his name¦119012.
\v 7 Then¦119018 he¦119018 told me¦119019, ‘You're¦119020 definitely pregnant¦119021 and you'll¦119020 \add ≈give birth to\add* a son¦119023. So¦119018 now¦119024, you mustn't drink wine¦119029 or strong¦119030 drink, and you mustn't eat¦119033 anything \add ≈prohibited by our rules\add*, because¦119037 the boy will¦119041 be¦119041 a Nazirite¦119039 to God¦119040 from¦119043 \add ≈conception\add* until¦119046 the day¦119048 of his death¦119049.’ ”
\p
\v 8 Then Manoah¦119053 prayed¦119052 to Yahweh¦119059, “Oh please, my¦119059 master¦119059, please let¦119064 the¦119060 man¦119060 of¦119060 God¦119061 that you¦119063 sent¦119063 \add before\add*, come¦119064 \add back\add* to us again¦119067 so¦119069 he¦119069 can instruct us about¦119059 what¦119070 we¦119072 must¦119072 do¦119072 for¦119073 this boy¦119073 who's¦119074 going to be born.”
\v 9 The¦119077 true God listened¦119076 to Manoah's¦119079 \add ≈request\add*, and¦119076 God's messenger¦119081 came¦119080 to the woman¦119086 again¦119083 when she¦119087 was¦119088 sitting¦119088 \add out\add* in¦119078 the field¦119089, \add ≈but¦119076\add* her husband¦119091 Manoah wasn't with¦119078 her,
\v 10 \add ≈so\add* \add *she\add* quickly¦119095 ran¦119097 and¦119095 told¦119098 her¦119099 husband¦119099, “Look¦119102. The¦119105 man¦119105 has¦119103 appeared \add again\add* who¦119106 came¦119108 to¦119110 me¦119110 that day¦119109.”
\v 11 \add ≈So¦119112\add* Manoah¦119114 \add ≈followed\add* his¦119116 wife, and¦119112 \add when\add* he came¦119117 to the man, he \add ≈asked\add* him¦119122, “Are¦119123 you¦119123 the man who¦119125 spoke¦119127 to \add ≈my wife\add* \add before\add*?”
\p “\add Yes,\add* \add ≈it¦119131 was me\add*,” he¦119117 replied¦119121.
\p
\v 12 “Now¦119136 may your¦119138 words¦119138 come¦119137 \add ≈true\add*,” said¦119134 Manoah¦119135. “\add ≈How should we bring up\add* the boy, and¦119145 \add ≈what¦119139 will¦119141 his¦119145 work¦119145 be\add*?”
\p
\v 13 “\add ≈You¦119152 wife must¦119160 carefully follow everything I¦119156 told her\add*,” Yahweh's¦119149 messenger¦119148 \add ≈replied¦119147\add*.
\v 14 “She mustn't \add ≈eat or drink\add* anything that comes¦119165 from¦119166 the grapevine¦119167. She mustn't drink wine or strong¦119171 drink, and¦119175 she mustn't eat anything \add ≈prohibited by our rules\add*. She must¦119169 do everything that I've instructed¦119184 her¦119184.”
\p
\v 15 “Please¦119195 \add ≈stay a little longer\add*,” Manoah¦119188 \add ≈asked\add* Yahweh's¦119192 messenger¦119191, “so¦119197 that¦119197 we¦119197 can prepare¦119197 a \add ≈meal of¦119191 tender goat¦119200 meat to¦119198 honour\add* your¦119198 presence.”
\p
\v 16 “Even if \add ≈I stayed longer\add*, I wouldn't eat¦119213 your¦119214 \add ≈food¦119214\add*,” Yahweh's¦119204 messenger \add ≈replied¦119202\add*. “\add ≈But¦119202\add* if you \add ≈wanted to¦119219\add* prepare a burnt¦119218 offering¦119218 for¦119219 Yahweh, you may offer¦119220 it¦119220.” (Because Manoah¦119207 didn't¦119211 know¦119224 that¦119226 \add ≈the¦119228 messenger had come from\add* Yahweh.)
\v 17 “What's¦119237 your name¦119239,” Manoah¦119233 \add ≈asked\add*, “so that we¦119245 can honour¦119245 you¦119245 when your words¦119243 come¦119242 \add ≈true\add*?”
\p
\v 18 “Why are¦119256 you¦119256 asking¦119256 about¦119257 my name¦119257,” Yahweh's¦119253 messenger¦119252 \add ≈replied¦119247\add*. “\add ≈You simply couldn't understand\add*.”
\p
\v 19 Then Manoah got their \add ≈young goat¦119271\add* and \add the other requirements for\add* the offering¦119274, and he¦119275 \add ≈presented\add* a sacrifice to Yahweh¦119279 on¦119276 the rock¦119278. Then \add ≈an amazing thing happened\add*\f + \fr 13:19 \ft We've changed this to a passive construction here, because it's not specified whether it was Yahweh himself or his messenger who did the miracle.\f* while Manoah and his¦119283 wife¦119283 watched—
\v 20 when¦119287 the \add #flames\add* went up into the sky from¦119289 the altar¦119290, Yahweh's¦119295 messenger¦119293 went up in¦119287 the \add #flames\add* while Manoah¦119298 and¦119286 his¦119299 wife¦119299 were¦119300 watching. \add ≈When¦119286 they saw that,\add* they \add ≈quickly bowed right down\add* to¦119305 the ground¦119305.
\v 21 \add After that,\add* Manoah and¦119307 his¦119319 wife¦119319 \add ≈never saw him again¦119310\add*, \add ≈but¦119307 now\add* Manoah knew¦119321 that he was¦119325 Yahweh's¦119312 messenger¦119325.
\p
\v 22 Manoah¦119330 said¦119329 to¦119331 his¦119333 wife¦119333, “\add ≈We'll definitely die now\add* because¦119336 we've¦119338 seen¦119338 God¦119337!”
\p
\v 23 But¦119340 his wife¦119342 replied¦119340 to him¦119341, “If¦119343 Yahweh¦119345 had¦119344 wanted to kill¦119346 us, he wouldn't have \add ≈accepted¦119349\add* the entire¦119357 burnt¦119351 offering and the grain¦119352 offering. He¦119354 wouldn't have shown¦119354 us all¦119357 those things, and he wouldn't have \add ≈given us those instructions\add*.”
\p
\v 24 \add In due course,\add* the¦119366 woman¦119366 gave¦119365 birth¦119365 to a son¦119367 and she¦119368 named¦119368 him Shimshon¦119372 \add (commonly misnamed as ‘Samson’ in English)\add*. The boy¦119374 grew¦119373 up and Yahweh¦119376 blessed¦119375 him,
\v 25 and then¦119378 Yahweh's¦119380 spirit¦119379 began¦119378 to¦119381 stir¦119381 him¦119381 \add when he was\add* in¦119382 Mahaneh¦119382 Dan—between Zorah¦119386 and between Eshtaol¦119388.
\c 14
\s1 Shimshon and the woman from Timnah
\rem /s1 Samson's Riddle; Samson's Marriage; Samson and the Woman from Timnah
\p
\v 1 \add One time when\add* Shimshon¦119392 went to¦119393 Timnah¦119393, he¦119394 \add ≈noticed¦119394\add* a young, Philistine woman¦119395 \add there\add*,
\v 2 and he went \add ≈back home\add* and he reported to his \add ≈parents¦119403\add*, “I saw¦119406 a Philistine woman¦119405 in¦119407 Timnah¦119407 \add ≈that I'd like you to get¦119411 for¦119414 me¦119414 as my\add* wife.”
\p
\v 3 “Isn't there¦119421 a woman¦119427 among your¦119423 relatives¦119423?” his \add ≈parents¦119420 asked\add* him, “Or among all¦119424 of¦119426 \add #our\add* people¦119426? Why take¦119432 a wife¦119433 from¦119434 the uncircumcised¦119435 Philistines¦119434?”
\p “Get¦119442 her for me,” Shimshon¦119437 said¦119436 to his father¦119440, “because¦119445 \add ≈I feel she's¦119448 right for me\add*.”
\p
\v 4 \add ≈Now¦119467 his¦119452 parents¦119452\add* didn't¦119453 know¦119454 that Yahweh¦119456 \add ≈was arranging this\add*, because¦119455 he¦119461 was seeking¦119463 an opportunity against¦119464 the Philistines¦119464 who were \add ≈oppressing\add* Yisrael¦119469 at¦119465 that time¦119465.
\p
\v 5 Then¦119481 Shimshon¦119473 went¦119477 to Timnah¦119476 with his¦119475 \add ≈parents¦119475\add* and¦119477 \add when\add* they¦119477 got to the¦119480 vineyards¦119480 \add *there\add*, wow, a young¦119483 lion¦119483 roared \add ≈when it saw\add* him¦119486.
\v 6 Yahweh's¦119491 spirit¦119490 \add ≈rose up in\add* \add @Shimshon\add*, and¦119488 he tore¦119492 \add @the lion\add* to pieces \add ≈with his bare hands¦119497\add* like butchering a young¦119494 goat¦119494, \add ≈but¦119488\add* he didn't¦119496 \add ≈mention to his parents¦119501\add* what he'd done¦119504.
\p
\v 7 Then¦119511 he \add ≈continued on\add* and talked with the¦119508 woman¦119508, and \add ≈he really liked her\add*.
\v 8 \add ≈Some¦119518 time¦119514 later when he returned¦119513 for the wedding\add*, he turned¦119516 off the path to look for the lion's carcass, and \add ≈to his surprise\add*, a swarm¦119522 of¦119522 bees¦119523 had nested in¦119524 the carcass and \add there was\add* honey¦119526 \add there\add*.
\v 9 He \add ≈filled his cupped hands¦119531 with honey¦119553\add*, and \add ≈continued\add* along—walking and eating¦119534 as he went¦119535 to \add ≈where his parents¦119541 were\add*. He even gave¦119542 some to them \add ≈to try\add*, but¦119528 he didn't¦119545 tell¦119547 them that¦119549 he had¦119552 scooped \add *it\add* out¦119552 of the¦119553 carcass¦119550 of a dead lion¦119551.
\v 10 Then¦119555 his¦119556 father¦119556 went \add ≈to¦119557 visit\add* the woman¦119559, and¦119560 \add ≈in the custom of the young¦119567 men¦119567\add* \add of the time\add*, Shimshon¦119562 \add ≈held\add* a party \add there\add*.
\v 11 \add ≈When¦119571\add* they saw¦119570 him, they brought thirty¦119573 close¦119574 friends¦119574 to join him.\f + \fr 14:11 \ft Although we can understand the words here, we're not certain of the implications, or perhaps the cultural traditions. Did Shimshon appear too alone? Too intimidating? Or was it just bringing more friends for good food and drink?\f*
\v 12 Samson¦119580 said¦119578 to them¦119579, “Let¦119581 me suggest a riddle¦119581 for¦119584 you all. If¦119586 you're \add able to\add* explain it to me during the seven¦119592 days¦119593 of the party and \add ≈you've worked it out¦119595 correctly\add*, I'll¦119596 give¦119596 you all thirty linen¦119599 coats and thirty sets of clothes¦119599.
\v 13 But¦119604 if¦119604 you all can't¦119606 \add ≈explain it\add* to me, then¦119604 you yourselves must¦119607 give¦119610 me thirty linen¦119614 coats and¦119604 thirty sets of¦119616 clothes¦119614.”
\p “\add ≈Okay, we're listening. Tell us the¦119620 riddle¦119621\add*.” they¦119618 \add ≈answered¦119618\add*.
\p
\v 14 \add ≈So¦119624\add* he¦119624 said¦119624,
\q1 “Food came out of the eater¦119626,
\q1 ≈and¦119624 sweetness¦119631 came out of the strong¦119629 one.”
\m But¦119624 three¦119636 days¦119637 later, they¦119633 \add ≈still couldn't solve his\add* riddle¦119635.
\p
\v 15 Then¦119639 on¦119665 the seventh¦119642 day¦119641,\f + \fr 14:15 \ft The Greek Septuagint translation says ‘the \+em fourth\+em* day’, which perhaps might fit better into the narrative, but ‘seventh’ is in the Hebrew that we have and not necessarily incorrect.\f* they¦119643 \add ≈pressured\add* Shimshon's¦119646 wife¦119644, “Get your husband¦119650 \add ≈to tell¦119651 you the answer of¦119644\add* the riddle¦119656, otherwise we'll burn¦119659 \add down\add* your father' house¦119663 and¦119639 you \add as well\add*. \add ≈Did you invite¦119667 us to the party just to impoverish¦119666\add* us?”
\p
\v 16 Then¦119671 Shimshon's¦119673 wife¦119672 \add ≈put on¦119674 a crying act\add* and¦119671 said¦119675, “You¦119678 must¦119696 hate¦119678 me—you don't¦119679 love¦119680 me \add because\add* you've¦119682 \add ≈created\add* a riddle¦119681 \add ≈for my relatives\add*, yet you haven't \add ≈even told¦119687 me the answer\add*.”
\p “Listen,” he \add ≈replied¦119675\add*, “I haven't \add even\add* \add ≈told my own parents¦119692\add*. Should I tell¦119696 you¦119680?”
\v 17 She¦119698 continued to cry beside him throughout the seven-day¦119700 party, and then¦119698 \add finally\add* on the seventh¦119700 day, he \add gave in and\add* told¦119711 her because¦119714 she kept nagging him, \add so in the end\add* she \add ≈was able to reveal the answer to her relatives\add*.
\v 18 Then¦119721 the men¦119723 of¦119723 the city¦119724 said¦119721 to¦119738 \add @Shimshon\add* before¦119727 the sun¦119729 went¦119728 down on¦119727 the seventh¦119726 day¦119725,
\q1 “What¦119730 is¦119732 sweeter¦119732 than¦119733 honey¦119733?
\q1 And¦119734 what's¦119734 stronger¦119735 than a lion¦119736?”
\m \add ≈So¦119721\add* he¦119737 said to them¦119738,
\q1 “If¦119739 you all hadn't ploughed with¦119727 my¦119741 heifer¦119741,
\q1 you wouldn't have¦119740 been able to solve my riddle¦119744.”
\p
\v 19 Then¦119746 Yahweh's¦119749 spirit¦119748 \add ≈rose up in\add* him¦119747, and he went to Ashkelon¦119751 and killed¦119752 thirty¦119755 men¦119756 \add there\add*. He took¦119757 their¦119760 possessions, and he gave¦119761 the changes¦119762 of clothes for the riddle¦119764. \add ≈But¦119746 he was very angry, so\add* he went \add ≈back to his parent's home\add*.
\v 20 Then \add (unknown to Shimshon)\add*, \add *his\add* wife¦119773 \add ≈was¦119777 given to the man who'd been\add* his close¦119775 friend¦119777 and¦119772 companion¦119775.
\c 15
\rem /s1 Samson's Vengeance on the Philistines; Samson Defeats the Philistines
\p
\v 1 Some days¦119781 later during the wheat¦119785 harvest¦119783, Shimshon¦119787 visited¦119786 his¦119790 wife with a young¦119791 goat¦119791 \add as a present for the family, because\add* he¦119793 said¦119793, “I'll go into¦119795 the bedroom with my wife.” \add ≈But¦119780\add* her¦119802 father¦119802 wouldn't allow him¦119801 to go in¦119782,
\v 2 and¦119805 \add ≈pleaded\add*, “Truly, I \add ≈I thought¦119808 that¦119809 you must\add* hate¦119811 her, so I gave¦119813 her to¦119814 your¦119814 close¦119814 friend¦119814. \add ≈But¦119805 wouldn't\add* her younger¦119817 sister¦119816 be better \add anyway\add*? Please¦119822, \add ≈would you take her instead¦119824\add*.”
\p
\v 3 Shimshon¦119828 \add ≈responded¦119826\add*, “This¦119830 time¦119830 I'm blameless concerning the¦119831 Philistines¦119831 when I \add ≈cause trouble for¦119827\add* them¦119836.”
\v 4 Then¦119839 \add *he\add* went¦119839 and captured¦119841 three¦119842 hundred¦119844 foxes¦119845 and \add ≈tied them in¦119860 pairs\add*—tail to¦119850 tail. He¦119840 took¦119846 torches¦119847, and \add ≈tied\add* one¦119855 torch¦119854 between¦119856 \add ≈the¦119858 tails¦119859 of¦119858 each pair\add*.
\v 5 He¦119862 \add ≈lit\add* the torches¦119865 and let \add @the foxes\add* loose into the grain \add ≈crops\add* of the Philistines¦119868. As a result, he burnt¦119869 both the standing¦119873 grain \add plants\add* and also the \add harvested\add* \add #stacks¦119870\add* of sheaves¦119870, and even a vineyard¦119876 and an olive¦119877 orchard.
\v 6 \add ≈So¦119879\add* the¦119880 Philistines¦119880 \add ≈asked\add*, “Who¦119881 did¦119882 this¦119883?”
\p “Shimshon,” they \add ≈replied¦119879\add*, “the son-in-law¦119886 of¦119886 the \add ≈man from Timnah\add*, because¦119888 he¦119889 took¦119889 \add @Shimshon's¦119885\add* wife¦119892 and gave¦119893 her to his¦119894 close¦119894 friend¦119894.” As a result, the Philistines went¦119895 \add to Timnah\add* and they burnt¦119897 \add both\add* \add @the woman¦119892\add* and her father¦119901.
\v 7 Then¦119904 Shimshon¦119906 told them¦119905, “Since you actually did¦119909 that¦119910 \add horrible thing\add*, I certainly \add ≈won't\add* stop until I've¦119914 avenged¦119914 myself¦119914 against you all.”
\v 8 Then¦119919 he attacked the Philistines and¦119919 \add ≈crippled many of them, before going to\add* Etam's¦119931 rock¦119930 where he lived¦119928 in¦119929 a cave.
\s1 Shimshon defeats the Philistines
\rem /s1 Samson Defeats the Philistines
\p
\v 9 \add ≈However\add*, the Philistine \add warriors\add* went¦119934 and camped¦119936 in¦119937 Yehudah¦119937, and spread¦119938 out¦119938 \add around the town of\add* Lehi¦119939.
\v 10 \add ≈But¦119941\add* the¦119942 men¦119942 of Yehudah¦119943 \add ≈asked\add* \add <them\add*, “Why¦119944 have¦119945 you all come¦119945 \add ≈here to attack\add* us?”
\p “We've come to \add ≈capture\add* Shimshon¦119951,” they \add ≈replied¦119941\add*. “To \add ≈give him some of his own back\add*.”
\v 11 Then¦119959 three¦119960 thousand¦119961 men¦119962 from Yehudah¦119963 went¦119959 down¦119959 to¦119964 the crevice¦119966 in Etam's¦119968 rock¦119967, and¦119959 told Shimshon¦119970, “Don't¦119971 you realise that¦119980 the Philistines¦119977 are ruling¦119975 over¦119976 us? \add ≈Why are you causing more trouble for\add* us?”
\p “Just¦119985 as¦119985 they've done to me¦119987,” he¦119983 \add ≈replied¦119969\add*, “so¦119988 I've¦119989 done to them¦119990.”
\p
\v 12 “We've come¦119995 \add ≈here to tie you up so we¦119995 can hand¦119997\add* you over to the Philistines¦119999,” they¦119992 said¦120000.
\p “\add Okay,\add*” Shimshon¦120002 \add ≈responded¦119992\add*. “\add But\add* promise me that you all won't \add ≈injure\add* me yourselves.”
\p
\v 13 “No¦120014, however, we'll certainly¦120023 tie you up,” they \add ≈replied¦120011\add*, “and we'll hand¦120022 you over to them. But¦120011 truly we won't¦120024 kill¦120025 you.” Then¦120011 they tied¦120026 him up with¦120022 two¦120027 new¦120029 ropes¦120028 and brought¦120030 him up from¦120031 the¦120033 \add #rocks¦120033\add*.
\p
\v 14 \add ≈When they'd taken him\add* as far as Lehi¦120040, the Philistines¦120041 shouted¦120045 \add triumphantly\add* \add ≈when they saw\add* him. Then Yahweh's¦120050 spirit¦120049 \add ≈rose in¦120060\add* \add @Shimshon\add* and the ropes¦120052 \add ≈around\add* his arms¦120056 \add ≈just seemed as weak as burnt¦120059\add* flax stalks \add ≈when he simply snapped them off¦120061 him\add*.
\v 15 He¦120066 saw a fresh¦120070 donkey's¦120069 jawbone¦120067 \add lying on the ground\add*, and he reached¦120071 out¦120071 his¦120072 hand¦120072 and \add ≈grabbed\add* it, and he slaughtered a thousand¦120077 \add Philistine\add* men¦120078 with¦120076 it.
\v 16 Then¦120080 Shimshon¦120081 \add ≈declared\add*,
\q1 “With¦120082 a donkey's jawbone, one heap¦120085, two¦120085 heaps¦120085,
\q1 ≈with¦120082 a donkey's jawbone, I've¦120088 slaughtered a thousand¦120089 men¦120090.”
\m
\v 17 When¦120092 he finished¦120093 \add ≈reciting that\add*, he hurled the¦120096 jawbone \add ≈away\add*, and¦120092 he called¦120098 that place¦120099 ‘Ramat¦120101-Lehi¦120102’ \add (which means ‘jawbone hill’)\add*.
\p
\v 18 Then¦120104 he¦120104 was¦120104 very¦120105 thirsty¦120104 and cried out to¦120107 Yahweh¦120109, “You yourself have¦120112 given¦120112 your servant¦120115 this great¦120119 victory¦120118, \add ≈but¦120104\add* now¦120121 must¦120122 I¦120122 die¦120122 with¦120113 this thirst¦120123 and \add ≈get captured by those\add* uncircumcised¦120126 \add Philistines\add*?”
\v 19 \add ≈So¦120128\add* God¦120129 split¦120128 open¦120128 the \add little\add* hollow¦120132 that's at¦120135 Lehi, and water¦120138 came out of it. When¦120128 he drank¦120139, his¦120141 \add ≈strength\add* returned¦120140 and he revived¦120142. Because of that, he called it ‘Eyn-Hakkore¦120150’ \add (which means ‘the fountain of the one who called out’)\add*, which is in¦120135 Lehi to this¦120155 day¦120154.
\v 20 And \add so\add* \add @Shimshon\add* led Yisrael¦120160 for twenty¦120163 years¦120164 at¦120161 the time¦120161 of¦120161 the Philistine \add oppression\add*.
\c 16
\s1 Shimshon carries away the gates
\rem /s1 Samson Removes Gaza's Gates; Samson and Delilah; Samson at Gaza
\p
\v 1 Samson¦120168 went to Gaza¦120169 \add city\add* and¦120170 he saw¦120170 a prostitute¦120174 woman¦120173 there¦120172 \add ≈so he went in to spend the night with\add* her¦120176.
\v 2 \add ≈But¦120184\add* the Gazites¦120178 \add ≈passed around the news\add*, “Shimshon's¦120182 \add ≈in town¦120192\add*.” So they encircled the city¦120192 gate¦120191 and¦120185 they laid¦120185 in wait¦120185 for him all¦120188 night. They¦120193 \add ≈stayed\add* quiet¦120193 the whole¦120188 night, saying, “We'll kill¦120202 him \add ≈when it gets light¦120200\add*.”
\v 3 Samson¦120206 \add ≈stayed with¦120213 her\add* until¦120207 the middle¦120209 of the night¦120210, then he got up. He¦120215 picked up both¦120220 the gates¦120217 including¦120205 the cross-bar and their posts¦120221, and hoisted them up onto his¦120229 shoulders¦120229, and he carried¦120230 them up to¦120231 the top¦120233 of the hill¦120234 which¦120235 is¦120236 in¦120213 front¦120238 of Hebron¦120239.
\s1 Shimshon and Delilah
\rem /s1 Samson and Delilah
\p
\v 4 \add ≈Sometime\add* after¦120243 that, \add @Shimshon\add* fell in love¦120246 with¦120248 a \add Philistine\add* woman¦120247 named Delilah¦120251 who \add lived in\add* the¦120248 Sorek¦120249 valley¦120248.
\v 5 The Philistine leaders¦120255 came¦120253 to her and \add ≈requested\add*, “Make him open up. \add ≈Find out what makes him strong, and how we can beat\add* him, so that we can tie him up \add ≈to keep him in¦120262 our power\add*. Then¦120253 we'll¦120268 each¦120274 give¦120271 you one thousand¦120275 one hundred¦120276 silver¦120277 coins.”
\p
\v 6 So¦120279 Delilah¦120280 \add ≈asked\add* Shimshon¦120283, “Please¦120286, tell¦120284 me¦120287 \add ≈what makes you so strong\add*, and¦120279 how \add ≈could someone tie you up to¦120293 keep you in¦120288 their power\add*?”
\p
\v 7 “If¦120298 they'd tie me¦120300 up with¦120301 seven¦120301 green cords¦120302 that¦120304 haven't been¦120307 dried¦120307,” \add *he¦120295\add* \add ≈replied¦120295\add*, “then¦120295 I'd become¦120310 weak¦120310 and¦120295 be just like¦120312 any other \add #man\add*.”
\p
\v 8 \add ≈So¦120315\add* the Philistine¦120319 leaders¦120318 brought¦120315 her seven¦120320 green cords¦120321 that¦120323 hadn't been¦120326 dried¦120326, and¦120327 she¦120327 tied¦120327 him¦120327 up with¦120328 them¦120328.
\v 9 Now she had an ambush¦120330 \add group\add* \add ≈ready\add* in¦120333 the \add next\add* room¦120333 when she said¦120334 to him¦120335, “The¦120336 Philistines¦120336 are¦120337 \add ≈here to get\add* you¦120337, Shimshon¦120338!” But¦120330 he¦120339 tore¦120339 the cords¦120342 to shreds just¦120343 like a strip of flax fiber¦120347 is torn¦120344 to shreds when \add ≈the fire¦120349 reaches it\add*, \add ≈thus\add* \add ≈they didn't¦120350 find out\add* \add the secret of\add* his¦120352 strength¦120352.
\v 10 Then¦120354 Delilah¦120355 said¦120354 to \add *him\add*, “Listen, you deceived¦120360 me and¦120354 lied to me. Now¦120365 tell¦120366 me, please¦120368, \add ≈how¦120370 could someone tie you up?\add*”
\p
\v 11 “Actually, if¦120375 they¦120378 would tie me¦120378 with¦120379 new¦120380 ropes¦120379 \add ≈which¦120381 have¦120384 never been¦120384 used\add*,” he¦120373 \add ≈replied¦120373\add*, “I'd become¦120387 weak¦120387 and¦120373 be just like¦120389 any other \add #man\add*.”
\p
\v 12 \add ≈So¦120392\add* Delilah¦120393 took¦120392 new¦120395 cords and¦120392 tied¦120396 him up with them. She¦120398 said¦120398 to him, “The¦120400 Philistines¦120400 are¦120401 \add ≈here to get\add* you¦120401, Shimshon¦120402!” with the ambush¦120403 group staying in¦120405 the \add next\add* room¦120405. But¦120392 he¦120406 flicked them off his¦120408 arms¦120408 like¦120409 they were \add just\add* thread¦120409.
\p
\v 13 Then¦120411 Delilah¦120412 said¦120411 to Shimshon¦120415, “\add ≈So far\add* you've¦120419 \add just\add* \add ≈been deceitful¦120419 to\add* me and¦120411 told me lies¦120423. \add Now\add* Tell¦120424 me how¦120426 you can be¦120427 tied¦120427 up.”
\p So he¦120428 told her¦120429, “\add It's\add* if¦120430 you¦120427 just weave¦120432 seven¦120435 locks of¦120436 my¦120437 hair into the¦120440 loom.”
